    "q-1251": {
      "dom_id": "step-q1251",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Express the compound inequality $x<-\\sqrt{2}$ or $x>\\sqrt{3}$ using interval notation.",
      "worked_solution": "We can represent the compound inequality on a number line, as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191664/assets/7a5c75d8772512cbf3fc25bc8b43d052ecb0a5c2737d55113f839c56b662424a.png)\n\nThe number line consists of the interval $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2})$ as well as the interval $(\\sqrt{3},\\infty ).$\n\nSo, we can express the compound inequality as the union of these two intervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2})\\cup (\\sqrt{3},\\infty )\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
