    "q-102117": {
      "dom_id": "step-q102117",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Express $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2}]\\cup (-1,\\infty )$ as an inequality.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we graph the two intervals on a number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191664/assets/65f772a179911c50e8159f2c24a2bfcc4a73ea00312ac84ced35627c635bc943.png)\n\nThe interval $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2}]$ can be expressed as the inequality $x\\le -\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nLikewise, the interval $(-1,\\infty )$ can be expressed as the inequality $x>-1.$\n\nSo, the union of these intervals represents the compound inequality\n\n$$\nx\\le -\\sqrt{2}\\text{ or }x>-1.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
